Changelog: 05-11-2026 17:31:18

Release 0.2.0.

Changelog: 05-11-2026 16:20:03

Fixed. The tests of the drive list no longer read the machine's /sys: with a USB drive plugged into the board as
sdc, the test of the USB stick saw that drive's link and failed. Only the tests changed, not the app.

Changelog: 05-11-2026 15:44:27

Added. The top row holds the health, the serial (hidden; a click shows it in a popup below its pill, so the pill
keeps its width, and it goes with another drive or Refresh), a speed test and Refresh. The list names the drives by
number (Drive 1, Drive 2), with the drive's model and size under it. Below, the drive's specs, a pill each: the power
cycles, the power-on hours and the power-on days (full days), the device, the data the host read and wrote, the
firmware, a firmware update from fwupd (Available, Up to date, Unknown or Not supported), the interface and the
link's speed (PCIe, SATA or USB, from sysfs). Two more pills say whether the board holds the drive back: the most
the board's port carries (orange when it is less than the drive can do) and the maker's rated read and write, from
a list of the NVMe drives of PCIe 3.0 and later of Samsung, WD_BLACK, Crucial and SK hynix (from the makers' data
sheets) and a few SATA SSDs. The speed test measures the read and write speed in this board with a test file of
1 GB, written, read back past the cache and deleted. Drives on USB are listed too, with their specs; without SMART
behind their adapter the state says No SMART over USB. An M.2 enclosure with a known NVMe bridge (Realtek RTL9210,
ASMedia ASM2362, JMicron JMS583) shows NVMe over USB; a USB drive stuck at USB 2.0 in a USB 3 port (its port's peer
on the USB 3 bus) turns the Speed pill orange with what to try, and Board link shows what the port can do.

Changelog: 05-11-2026 03:50:19

Release 0.1.0.

Changelog: 05-11-2026 02:36:25

Added. SSD Check: the health, power cycles and power-on hours of each drive that reports SMART, read from udisks
over D-Bus without root (busctl). An NVMe drive gives all three (health is 100 minus its percentage used); a SATA SSD
gives its hours and cycles and its health from the first known maker's attribute (231, 169, 202, 233, 177), else
"Not reported". The state beside the name shows a problem the drive itself flags; with more drives a list chooses
one. The drive's serial number stays out of the window. Written by tools/new_app.py from the architecture chapter.
